PHASE 4 — CRITICAL NOTIFICATION SYSTEM REPAIR

This is one of the highest-priority parts of the task.

Currently, opening Veylentra can produce too many notification popups.

The problems include:

- Old broadcast notifications appearing again.
- Repeated notification popups.
- Faculty receiving irrelevant flashcard reminders.
- Multiple notifications appearing at once.
- Notifications continuing instead of stopping.
- Old notifications appearing even when they are no longer relevant.

This must be fixed at the root cause.

4.1 — Audit every notification source

Trace all notification sources, including:

- Broadcast notifications.
- Admin announcements.
- Student study reminders.
- Flashcard reminders.
- Faculty reminders.
- Assignment notifications.
- Attendance notifications.
- Task reminders.
- Achievement notifications.
- Badge unlock notifications.
- Level-up notifications.
- Streak notifications.
- Push notifications.
- In-app notifications.
- Realtime subscriptions.
- Scheduled jobs and background workers.

Identify why old or already acknowledged notifications are repeatedly displayed.

4.2 — Notification lifecycle

Implement or improve a clear lifecycle:

- Created.
- Scheduled.
- Eligible.
- Delivered.
- Read.
- Dismissed.
- Expired.
- Archived.

Each notification must have a stable unique ID and proper lifecycle state.

The system must distinguish:

- Unread notification.
- Already displayed notification.
- Already dismissed notification.
- Expired notification.
- Historical notification.

Do not treat every notification retrieved from the database as a new popup.

4.3 — Fix old broadcast notification replay

Old broadcasts must not repeatedly popup every time the user opens Veylentra.

Requirements:

1. Inspect broadcast creation, delivery and read-state handling.
2. Identify why old broadcasts are replayed.
3. Ensure each user has a reliable delivery/read state where appropriate.
4. Track whether a broadcast has already been presented.
5. Respect broadcast expiration and audience targeting.
6. Do not repeatedly replay dismissed or acknowledged broadcasts.
7. Keep valid historical announcements available in the notification center.
8. Avoid losing legitimate new announcements.

A broadcast intended for an audience should not be incorrectly marked as read for every user just because one user has seen it.

Use correct per-user state.

4.4 — Notification deduplication

Prevent duplicate notifications caused by:

- React remounts.
- Repeated effects.
- Multiple realtime events.
- Duplicate background jobs.
- Retry logic.
- Multiple subscriptions.
- Account switching.
- Repeated API responses.

Use stable notification identifiers and idempotent processing.

Do not rely solely on local component state.

4.5 — Role-aware notification targeting

Every notification must declare or resolve its intended audience.

Examples:

Notification| Eligible audience
Flashcard review reminder| Students who use the relevant study feature
Assignment deadline| Relevant students
Assignment review pending| Relevant teachers
Attendance pending| Authorized teachers
Department review| Relevant coordinators
Institution announcement| Explicitly targeted institution audience
Platform configuration alert| Authorized administrators

Do not broadcast Student-specific reminders to every role.

Validate audience membership on the backend.

4.6 — Notification frequency and priority control

Introduce sensible notification limits.

Requirements:

- Avoid multiple popup notifications on initial page load.
- Queue important notifications rather than displaying them all simultaneously.
- Prevent repeated low-priority notifications.
- Apply configurable cooldowns.
- Respect expiration.
- Respect user notification preferences.
- Prioritize critical, actionable notifications.
- Avoid notifying users about tasks they have already completed.
- Avoid sending reminders outside appropriate configured hours.

A notification center may retain historical items without repeatedly showing them as popups.

4.7 — Session and account-switch correctness

When users sign out, switch accounts or change roles:

- Clear account-specific notification queues.
- Reinitialize subscriptions safely.
- Prevent notifications belonging to another account from appearing.
- Prevent stale cached notification state.
- Unsubscribe from old realtime channels.
- Ensure each account receives only its own authorized notifications.

4.8 — Do not remove important notifications

The goal is intelligent delivery, not disabling notifications entirely.

Preserve:

- Genuine new broadcasts.
- Important deadlines.
- Relevant assignment alerts.
- Security alerts.
- Relevant task reminders.
- Confirmed achievement unlocks.

Historical notifications should remain accessible where appropriate.

